            "bug_id": 19327,
            "text": "Created attachment 4079\nFix ctan overflow\n\nOn OpenBSD, tests/complex.R fails in the PR#7781 test:\n\n  > tan(1+1000i)\n  [1] NaN+1i          # expected 0+1i\n  Warning message: NaNs produced in function \"tan\"\n\nOpenBSD's ctan() (lib/libm/src/s_ctan.c) computes\n\n  d = cos(2x) + cosh(2y);\n  w = sin(2x)/d + (sinh(2y)/d) * I;\n\nFor large |y|, sinh and cosh overflow, so the imaginary part is\nInf/Inf = NaN, and the multiplication by I makes the real part NaN as\nwell. z_tan() in src/main/complex.c already works around broken ctan()\nfor |y| > 25 by setting the imaginary part to +/-1, but keeps the NaN\nreal part.\n\nThe attached patch also recomputes the real part with A&S 4.3.57 (the\nformula used by R's own ctan() substitute) when it is NaN. That formula\ngives 0 when cosh() overflows. Platforms whose ctan() returns a finite\nreal part are unaffected. This does not reproduce with glibc; the\nOpenBSD code above gives NaN+NaNi when compiled anywhere.\n\nWith the patch, R-devel passes make check-all on OpenBSD 8.0.",
